            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "json",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Molecular.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Molecular.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 7,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-octet_rule",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The octet rule: atoms bond to reach eight valence electrons. Carbon has 4 of its own and shares 4 more, 4 + 4 = 8 — a full outer shell, the driver of covalent bonding."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "4 + 4 = 8"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem octet_rule : 4 + 4 = 8 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-bond_shares_electron_pairs",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A covalent bond of order n shares 2n electrons: single, double and triple bonds share 2, 4 and 6 — [1,2,3] → [2,4,6]. The bond IS the shared pair(s)."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(([1,2,3] : List Nat).map (fun n => 2 * n)) = [2,4,6]"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem bond_shares_electron_pairs : (([1,2,3] : List Nat).map (fun n => 2 * n)) = [2,4,6] := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-bond_order_n2_o2",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Bond order is (bonding − antibonding)/2: N₂ gets (8−2)/2 = 3 (a triple bond) and O₂ gets (8−4)/2 = 2 (a double bond). Nitrogen holds three shared pairs, oxygen two."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((8 - 2) / 2 = 3) ∧ ((8 - 4) / 2 = 2)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem bond_order_n2_o2 : ((8 - 2) / 2 = 3) ∧ ((8 - 4) / 2 = 2) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-valence_from_group",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Main-group valence electrons are the group number minus 10: carbon (group 14) has 4, oxygen (group 16) has 6 — 14 − 10 = 4 and 16 − 10 = 6. Valence count sets how many bonds an atom forms."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(14 - 10 = 4) ∧ (16 - 10 = 6)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem valence_from_group : (14 - 10 = 4) ∧ (16 - 10 = 6) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-water_lewis_electrons",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A Lewis structure counts total valence electrons: H₂O has 2·1 (the hydrogens) + 6 (oxygen) = 8 electrons — four pairs, two bonding and two lone. The dot structure conserves the count."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "2 * 1 + 6 = 8"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem water_lewis_electrons : 2 * 1 + 6 = 8 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-ionic_threshold",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A large electronegativity difference makes a bond ionic: NaCl has |3.0 − 0.9| = 2.1 (×10: 30 − 9 = 21), above the ~1.7 (×10: 17) ionic threshold — 21 > 17. The more electronegative atom takes the electron outright."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(30 - 9 > 17) ∧ (3 % 9 = 3)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem ionic_threshold : (30 - 9 > 17) ∧ (3 % 9 = 3) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-molar_mass_water",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Molar mass sums the atomic masses: water is 2·1 (hydrogen) + 16 (oxygen) = 18 g/mol. The molecule weighs exactly its parts."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "2 * 1 + 16 = 18"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem molar_mass_water : 2 * 1 + 16 = 18 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
